# Rank 1517 / CVE-2026-45785: OpenMcdf descended a compound-file directory
# tree using only per-hop ordering checks, so a crafted sibling cycle hung
# the process (loop with unreachable exit, CWE-835). The directory entry
# tree in a CFB is a red-black-ish binary search tree stored on disk:
# TryGetDirectoryEntry in OpenMcdf/DirectoryTree.cs walked
# `while (child is not null) { compare = Compare(nameSpan, child.NameCharSpan);
# ... child = directories.TryGetSibling(child, siblingType, validateColor); }`
# where the old TryGetSibling in OpenMcdf/DirectoryEntries.cs validated only
# the immediate name order (Left must compare < 0, Right > 0) and optionally
# the red-black colour rule -- and every caller passed validateColor=false.
# TryGetParent and the insert walk were worse: they descended through raw
# TryGetDictionaryEntry(child.LeftSiblingId, out child) with no check at all.
# A mixed-direction cycle satisfies per-hop ordering (A right B, B left C,
# C right A with names consistent at each hop), so the loop never exits;
# only the enumerator had Brent's cycle detection, the search paths had
# none. The fixing commit c6f82db72 ("Enforce binary search tree
# validation", citing GHSA-5qwm-7pvp-w988, resolving the nocommit flag --
# the advisory names no commit but the commit message names the advisory):
# sibling access now goes through an IDirectoryTreeValidator; the new
# DirectoryTreeSearchOrderValidator carries a [min,max] name window down
# the descent so any cycle must cross a bound and throw, the new
# DirectoryTreeTraversalOrderValidator does the per-hop checks for
# enumeration, and the enumerator's own Brent check is removed as
# redundant.
#
# ATTENTION, structural-loop termination vocabulary. The weakness is a
# loop whose exit depends on a structural invariant of attacker-supplied
# data that the code never establishes -- no tainted value reaches a
# sink, and the fix adds an invariant check rather than a bound. The
# corpus's CWE-835 family reaches one edge of the fix -- VYQL-DOS-029
# (code.NumericParserMissingProgressCheck) and VYQL-DOS-028
# (code.BufReadDiscardLoopWithoutEofGuard) judge missing-progress loops,
# exactly this judgement for parsers and buffered reads -- but the other
# edge is missing: no concept names a descent over a linked structure,
# so no rule can ask whether it carries a cycle guard, and a binding on
# OpenMcdf's own TryGetSibling would detect this library rather than the
# weakness.
#
# ## Recheck measurements
#
# Re-probed at the recheck span tip, which carries every definition and
# engine change shipped since the first pass. Path 1 does not convert:
# the ontology still has no concept for a descent over a linked structure
# (the nearest, RecursiveObjectParentResolveWithoutCycleGuard, is a
# CWE-674 recursion concept fed by one PHP binding about dictionary
# Parent resolution, not an iterative sibling walk), VYQL-DOS-028/029
# are fed only by the rust and c bindings for buffered reads and numeric
# parsers, and the C# frontend's analysis observations cover JWT and CAS
# flags only (csSecurityObservations, csharp.go) -- nothing observes a
# loop. Measured 3x --cache off / timeout 180 / --max-ram 6GB per
# revision on the real checkout, vulnerable parent c6f82db72^ against
# fix c6f82db72:
#
#   OpenMcdf/DirectoryTree.cs, DirectoryEntries.cs,
#   DirectoryTreeEnumerator.cs -- 0/0/0 findings each at the vulnerable
#   revision, full findings view, deterministic; vyql match on
#   DirectoryTree.cs labels nothing at all: no source, no sink, no
#   control. A hard zero, not a misread one.
#   whole repository -- 30/30/30 findings at both revisions (csharp:133
#   at the parent, 136 at the fix): 18 vs 19 VYQL-NUM-005
#   UncheckedEncodedLengthArithmetic, 9 VYQL-RES-001 ResourceLeak, one
#   VYQL-MOB-006, one VYQL-CFG-014. The +1 NUM-005 is the fix's own
#   added validator files, nothing on any descent. The flags view on
#   OpenMcdf/ reports the same 15 findings plus 36 flags, all
#   core.ResourceRelease controls.
#
# Path 3 is not shippable today, measured on this very tree: the
# descent shape occurs twice in OpenMcdf/ (the search walk at
# DirectoryTree.cs:35 and PushLeft at DirectoryTreeEnumerator.cs:89),
# and PushLeft's own class carries Brent's cycle detection at the
# vulnerable revision -- a guard one method away that a function-scoped
# rule cannot see. Worse, the fix DELETES Brent's check as redundant and
# replaces it with a stack-top order invariant, so the obvious rule shape
# "descent without a visited-set or two-pointer guard" flags the
# remediation and clears the bug. What the rule needs is loop-carried
# guard reasoning (visited-set, depth bound, or order-window tracking),
# which no frontend's per-statement guard families provide; the entry
# filed at the first pass stands. No definition change shipped. Spec
# cve_rank1517 records all four descents and their guarded forms, all
# asserting reject VYQL-DOS-029: when structural-descent vocabulary
# lands, the unguarded walks must flip to expect.

test "rank1517: the directory search descends sibling links with per-hop checks only"
  lang csharp
  file OpenMcdf/DirectoryTree.cs
  reject VYQL-DOS-029
  code
  ```
  internal sealed class DirectoryTree
  {
      private readonly DirectoryEntries directories;
      private readonly DirectoryEntry root;

      public bool TryGetDirectoryEntry(string name, out DirectoryEntry entry)
      {
          if (!directories.TryGetDictionaryEntry(root.ChildId, out DirectoryEntry child))
          {
              entry = null;
              return false;
          }

          while (child is not null)
          {
              int compare = DirectoryEntryComparer.Compare(name, child.Name);
              if (compare == 0)
              {
                  entry = child;
                  return true;
              }

              SiblingType siblingType = compare < 0 ? SiblingType.Left : SiblingType.Right;
              child = directories.TryGetSibling(child, siblingType, false);
          }

          entry = null;
          return false;
      }
  }
  ```

